        "body": "South Carolina's tracked 44 bills for the 2025 to 2026 session cover an unusually wide range of AI specific topics: a Right to Compute Act, an AI open source and decentralization bill, two separate Protecting Children from Chatbots bills, two Chatbot Regulation bills, an AI and Therapy or Psychotherapy bill, a Personhood bill, and a Digital Choice Act filed in both House and Senate versions.\n\nSeveral bill pairs, Stop Harm from Addictive Social Media, Protecting Children from Chatbots, Chatbot Regulation, and Digital Choice Act, each appear once in the House and once in the Senate with matching titles, the standard way companion legislation moves through a two chamber legislature, each chamber advancing its own version of the same policy simultaneously.\n\nFiling both a Right to Compute Act and an AI open source and decentralized bill in the same session frames AI policy here, as in Ohio, partly around access to computing resources and open infrastructure rather than only around content harms, while the personhood bill and the likeness, voice, and image security act address a different question entirely, what legal status AI systems and their outputs have relative to real people.\n\nThe data does not indicate which of these 44 bills passed, failed, or remain pending, nor does it include bill text beyond title and number, so South Carolina's actual enacted AI framework is not established by this record.",
